<p><strong>Approach #3: splitting out not-so-hands-on languages and technol=
ogies</strong></p>
<p>The downside of having a list of languages and technologies is that it d=
oes not differentiate between ones that you are hands-on with, and ones whe=
re you would need a refresher. In this case, adding technologies that you a=
re a bit more rusty with=E2=80=94but differentiating these=E2=80=94can be a=
n option.</p>
<p>Here=E2=80=99s an example of this, where a person is applying for a posi=
tion for a company that is heavy on Ruby. They=E2=80=99ve done this in the =
past and wouldn=E2=80=99t mind picking it up again, but their Ruby knowledg=
e is not on the same level as JavaScript and Java, which they both use day-=
to-day.</p>
